WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
Hi guys, I'm Eric Voss, and Rogue One: A Star Wars Story is now in theaters

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.140
and so much about this movie blew me away

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.780
but for right now I just want to talk about one thing in particular:

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.100
the visual effects and the surprisingly good CGI

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.400
that was used to re-create certain characters from the original Star Wars film and

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.400
obviously I'm about to get into some major spoilers so

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.580
you should probably just go watch Rogue One first

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.660
but honestly this story, and the characters that I'm

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.080
going to talk about, have all been around for like

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.680
forty years now! So, what? Am I supposed to Not talk about this?

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.840
Come on! This is my Life! This is All I have!

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.240
Okay, so Rogue One tells the story of the events that led up to the

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.760
original Star Wars: A New Hope. Literally catching us up

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.040
to the exact moment that Princess Leia

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.580
receives the Death Star plans that she hands off to
R2-D2 at the beginning of that movie

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.520
And I don't know about you guys, but when the opening

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.000
night audience that I watched the movie with

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.360
saw the face of Princess Leia, Carrie Freakin' Fisher herself

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.160
but like, forty years younger somehow? We

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.700
lost our freaking minds!

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.320
There was this massive gasp, and no joke,

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.300
I literally heard someone whisper:

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.120
*It's Magic*! ...It was Me! I said that.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.460
...I shouted it, actually. But

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.240
the thing is, it's not magic

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.500
it's just really impressive, and probably super expensive

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.300
visual effects, by George Lucas's special effects

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.480
and animation studio Industrial Light and Magic.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.500
So, I guess magic was kind of part of it.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.740
And obviously Rogue One did the same thing

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.460
to recreate Grand Moff Tarkin, originally played by

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.140
Peter Cushing, who died in 1994

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.380
as well as the Gold leader and the Red leader

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.920
in the Battle of Scarif.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.840
So how did the filmmakers pull this off?

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.880
It's a complicated process, that uses a mix of

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.060
body double actors and computer generated mapping

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.000
of archived footage over the stand-in's face.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.940
Using motion capture and re-animating it all to

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.120
make the facial expressions and move the lips

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.220
in a way to make them speak the new dialogue.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.020
“We're mutants Jean, we are like you.”

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.840
And for this to look even close to authentic

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.080
you need an actor who can closely match the original

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.120
actor's facial structure, expressions, gestures...

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.460
Just pretty much do the perfect full body impression of them.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.100
And you may remember the video that we did when a

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.120
similar process was used, to finish the late

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.020
Paul Walker's scenes in  Fast and the Furious VII

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.060
and in that case, Paul Walker's brothers

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.180
stepped in as body doubles and

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.700
the finished product was pretty good.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.940
But this was not easy to do for Tarkin.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.960
Last year, rumors from a source close to

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.160
Rogue One called it one of the most

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.540
complex and costly CGI re-creations ever.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.940
Now, apparently when George Lucas filmed

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.620
the first Star Wars, Peter Cushing didn't feel

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.920
comfortable wearing the awkward leather riding boots

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.980
that the Imperial officers had to wear,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.460
and he complained so much that George Lucas

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.780
allowed him to wear slippers on set, and he

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:34.720
just shot him from the knees up, or

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.640
from behind the Death Star conference table.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.520
So, for Rogue One they didn't have a lot of footage

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.760
options to work with, and they had to rely on some

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.640
of Cushing's previous work on horror films.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.840
And by the way, that might be why this version

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.540
of Tarkin that we saw briefly

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.200
in Revenge of the Sith looked, kinda weird.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.060
So, they hired British actor Guy Henry.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.400
You may remember him as 
the corrupt minister of magic

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.840
in the final two Harry Potter films,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.800
or, if you're into BBC hospital soap operas,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.220
Dr. Henrik Hanssen on Holby City...

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.260
So this Guy has a very similar long, angular face

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.980
as Peter Cushing, and the way he talks is eerily

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.240
similar. “You have nothing to fear” … and

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.480
if you look closely at the Star Wars celebration reel

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.580
footage that came out last summer,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.540
you can see Guy Henry on set

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.880
before he was transformed into Tarkin. And in case

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.940
you were curious, that's actually Guy Henry doing Tarkin's voice.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.000
“You have made time an ally of the rebellion.”

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.200
A source close to the film actually confirmed this for us.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.420
Now for Princess Leia, they used young Norwegian actress

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.880
notice that she has a strikingly similar, round, full face

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.180
and round eyes that Carrie Fisher had when

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.480
she was twenty years old.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.980
Now, Deila hasn't appeared in much other than a minor roll in Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.160
So then it was a matter of going through hours

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.120
and hours of archived footage, and using it all to

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.620
create 3-D animations of Peter Cushing's and Carrie Fisher's faces

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.620
to map over the body doubles,
rendering them frame by frame

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.840
with realistic skin textures to make it all seamless.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.960
And in addition to Fast and Furious VII

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.200
you may remember this effect being used recently to make actors look younger

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.100
Like Anthony Hopkins in Westworld and

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.200
Robert Downey Jr. in Captain America: Civil War.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.940
Actually Industrial Light and Magic did the effects for Civil War too

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.700
And the animators on that film talked about how

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.200
ILM has this massive library of image scans

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.480
to use as references.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.340
And knowing George Lucas, that library probably includes

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.660
all kinds of footage and angles, and digital re-masters

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:28.460
and he's probably like, [high voice] "Ohhh, I got Ani and Jar-Jar too, if you guys need it!...Uh, no? Oh, okay."

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.280
In Rogue One all the effects were overseen by Oscar winning visual effects wizard John Knoll

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.360
of Industrial Light and Magic. This guy is one of the best.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.540
He did all the Star Wars prequels and the Pirates of the Caribbean movies

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.180
a ton of stuff. In fact, it was John Knoll who originally pitched the initial idea for Rogue One's story.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.420
And obviously, a lot of the success of this movie

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.600
depended on Knoll's confidence and

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.040
his team's ability to authentically re-create

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.620
Tarkin and Leia, to have that payoff when we see

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.640
how this story fits in with the original Star Wars.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.420
And according to director Gareth Edwards

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.560
"...after a lot of blood, sweat and tears..."

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.220
and Knoll assuring them "We can do this, we can do it..."

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.580
I think they pulled it off.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.640
Now, Tarkin didn't look 100% authentic

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.480
especially the movement of his mouth, but...

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:10.240
he looked better than any other time this has been used

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.560
And to be honest, I couldn't see any difference at all with Leia

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.000
And maybe that's just because as a younger actress at the time, Carrie Fisher had fewer wrinkles

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.340
and blemishes on her face,
a lot less detail than Peter Cushing had as Tarkin

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.400
and so it was easier to kinda recreate that.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.100
And whether or not this looked believable to you

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.920
you've got to admit, it ended up being a huge part of what made Rogue One so enjoyable

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.840
That cold, menacing presence of Tarkin
as they have him say

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.240
“You may fire when ready.”

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.420
And then that closing image of a young, radiant
Carrie Fisher, saying “Hope.”

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.800
as the story moves right into ‘A New Hope’.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.680
Now, I know using old footage like this
can come off as somewhat in poor taste

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.960
especially when the actor has passed away,
as in Cushing's case

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:50.640
and some people were saying this 
took them out of the movie a little bit

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.280
kinda like they were bringing the actor back from the dead and it looked a little video gamey

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.500
and they should have just cast a similar looking actor

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.320
Like Genevieve O'Reilly as Mon Mothma

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.960
but just to be clear, the film did get a blessing
and the rights to use Cushing's likeness

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.320
from his family and estate.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.000
They also credited Carrie Fisher.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.780
Not sure about Garven Dreis or Jon Vander though.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.960
and sure, it's fair to be concerned about
what this could lead to in filmmaking

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.480
like it's possible to get to a point where every actor's past performances are repurposed

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.140
and distorted in a way that they didn't initially agree to when they signed on to make the film

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.920
But honestly, so far, that hasn't really happened.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.320
Grand Moff Tarkin and Princess Leia
were brought back in a specific way

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.340
by the same digital effects company

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:30.780
to complete the story as the characters were
in the original film

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:33.240
while giving Tarkin's cruelty even more depth.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.780
That's not changing the character

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.060
and it's definitely not Greedo shooting first.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.880
Alright, keep an eye out for other 
Rogue One videos that we're puttin' out.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.620
I'm gonna talk about how exactly Rogue One's story connects to A New Hope

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.600
and I'm planning a larger, super in-depth breakdown

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.120
of all the Easter eggs and references to
the Star Wars universe in this movie

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.720
because there were SO freakin' many.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.800
It's gonna take me at least like five screenings
to catch all of 'em.

